.TH NAUTHILUS-DIRECTORCTL 1 "2026-06-16" "nauthilus-director" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nauthilus-directorctl \- control a running Nauthilus Director
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nauthilus-directorctl
.RB [ --address
.IR url ]
.RB [ --timeout
.IR duration ]
.RB [ --output
.IR text|json ]
.RB [ --auth-bearer-token-file
.IR path ]
.RB [ --tls-client-cert
.IR path ]
.RB [ --tls-client-key
.IR path ]
.RB [ --help ]
.IR command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nauthilus-directorctl
is the operator CLI for the Director control API.
Every HTTP request is sent through the generated OpenAPI client SDK.
The default output mode is compact key-value text for scripts.
JSON output returns generated response bodies where practical.
Use
.B --help
on the root command or any nested command to list available subcommands and
command-local options.
Long options require a double dash; single-dash options are reserved for
documented one-letter shorthand flags.
.PP
Mutation commands manage runtime state in the running Director.
They do not edit or rewrite YAML configuration files.
There is no hidden compatibility migration or active-session deletion path for
old development Redis keys.
For demo and development resets, stop the affected Director processes and clear
or expire the isolated runtime keys outside the CLI.
.PP
Listener runtime control is process-local in v1.
A listener drain or resume command affects only the Director process addressed
by
.B --address
and does not fan out across a cluster.
Drain state is runtime-only and is not persisted after process restart; the
typed configuration snapshot remains the source of listener transport truth.
.PP
Production runbooks for control-plane security, Nauthilus OIDC caller auth,
reload, failure diagnosis and migration workflows are maintained under
.BR docs/operations/ .
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR --address " " \fIurl\fR
Control API base URL.
The default is
.BR http://127.0.0.1:9090 .
That compiled default is correct only for a plain HTTP control listener.
A bare host:port value is interpreted as HTTP.
Use an explicit
.B https://
URL when the control listener has TLS enabled.
.TP
.BR --timeout " " \fIduration\fR
Request timeout as a Go duration.
The default is
.BR 5s .
.TP
.BR --output " " \fItext|json\fR
Select output mode.
.TP
.BR --auth-bearer-token-file " " \fIpath\fR
Read a control-plane bearer token from a mounted secret file and send it as
.B Authorization: Bearer
on generated OpenAPI client requests.
The file value is trimmed only for line endings and is never printed.
.TP
.BR --auth-bearer-token " " \fItoken\fR
Send an inline control-plane bearer token.
This option exists for explicit operator use and tests; prefer
.B --auth-bearer-token-file
or
.B NAUTHILUS_DIRECTORCTL_BEARER_TOKEN_FILE
for production shells.
The token value is never included in command output or error messages.
.TP
.BR --tls-ca-file " " \fIpath\fR
Use the given CA bundle for the control API TLS connection.
.TP
.BR --tls-client-cert " " \fIpath\fR
Use the given client certificate for mTLS control-plane authentication.
It must be paired with
.BR --tls-client-key .
.TP
.BR --tls-client-key " " \fIpath\fR
Use the given client private key for mTLS control-plane authentication.
It must be paired with
.BR --tls-client-cert .
.TP
.BR --tls-server-name " " \fIname\fR
Override the TLS server name used for control API verification.
.TP
.B --tls-insecure-skip-verify
Disable control API server certificate verification.
This is a compatibility or emergency option and should not be used for
production control-plane access.
.PP
Bearer authentication may also be configured with
.B NAUTHILUS_DIRECTORCTL_BEARER_TOKEN_FILE
or
.BR NAUTHILUS_DIRECTORCTL_BEARER_TOKEN .
Configuring both inline and file-based bearer token input is rejected before
any request is sent.
.PP
For an HTTPS control listener with static bearer or OIDC bearer control auth,
use an explicit address and trust source, for example:
.RS
.nf
nauthilus-directorctl \\
  --address https://127.0.0.1:9090 \\
  --tls-ca-file /etc/nauthilus-director/control-ca.pem \\
  --auth-bearer-token-file /run/nauthilus-director/operator-control-token \\
  sessions list
.fi
.RE
.PP
For mTLS control auth, pair
.B --tls-client-cert
with
.B --tls-client-key
and keep
.B --tls-ca-file
or
.B --tls-server-name
configured for server verification.
.B --tls-insecure-skip-verify
is for emergency diagnostics only and is not a production recommendation.
.PP
Transport diagnostics distinguish invalid local address or TLS configuration,
plain HTTP sent to an HTTPS listener, TLS verification failure, connection
refusal or timeout, authentication and authorization failures, non-JSON
responses and structured runtime API problem responses.
Response body hints are bounded and redact common credential markers.
.PP
Read commands such as
.B sessions list
,
.B users list
and
.B users sessions
are authenticated control API reads, not local Redis inspection.
When the addressed control listener uses HTTPS or authentication, pass the same
explicit
.B https://
address, TLS verification flags and bearer-token-file or mTLS flags as for
mutating commands.
.TP
.B --version
Print the client version and exit.
.TP
.B --help
Print command help and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Show health, readiness, binary version and API version.
.TP
.B backends list
List backend runtime state.
The text output includes only secret-safe backend identity and transport
diagnostics: backend identifier, backend node, protocol, backend pool, shard tag,
outbound PROXY protocol status and runtime health or maintenance state.
It does not print backend socket addresses, TLS key paths or credential material.
Outbound PROXY protocol read-back is a boolean endpoint property; no configured
PROXY protocol version is exposed by the current REST contract.
.TP
.BI "backends show " identifier
Show one backend.
.TP
.BI "backends maintenance enable " identifier " --reason " text
Enable soft maintenance by default.
Use
.BI "--mode " soft|hard
to select maintenance mode and
.BI "--grace-seconds " seconds
when hard maintenance needs an explicit grace value.
.TP
.BI "backends maintenance disable " identifier " --reason " text
Disable backend maintenance.
.TP
.BI "backends out " identifier " --reason " text
Exclude a backend from new runtime placement.
.TP
.BI "backends in " identifier " --reason " text
Return a backend to runtime placement.
.TP
.BI "backends drain " identifier " --mode " soft|hard " --reason " text
Drain a backend.
Use
.BI "--grace-seconds " seconds
to send an explicit grace value.
.TP
.BI "backends weight " identifier " --weight " value " --reason " text
Override the runtime placement weight for one backend.
Use
.B 0
to exclude the backend from new weighted initial placement without terminating
active pins.
.TP
.BI "backends runtime weight " identifier " --weight " value " --reason " text
Alias for
.BR "backends weight" .
.TP
.BI "backends runtime clear " identifier " --reason " text
Clear runtime overrides for one backend.
.TP
.B listeners list
List configured process-local frontend listeners and their runtime state.
.TP
.BI "listeners show " name
Show one configured process-local frontend listener.
.TP
.BI "listeners drain " name " --mode " soft|hard " --reason " text
Drain one process-local frontend listener without editing YAML configuration.
Soft drain closes only the accept socket for new frontend connections and
leaves active local sessions running.
Hard drain requires an explicit
.BI "--grace-seconds " seconds
value, including
.B 0
for immediate local session closure intent.
.TP
.BI "listeners resume " name " --reason " text
Resume one process-local frontend listener from the running Director's current
configuration snapshot.
Resume rebinds the listener from the current typed configuration and does not
persist or clear any YAML setting.
.TP
.B config dump -d|--defaults
Read canonical default configuration from the running Director.
.TP
.B config dump -n|--non-default
Read non-default effective configuration from the running Director.
.TP
.B config dump -d|-n -P|--protected
Request protected config output from the running Director by sending the
generated protected config request flag.
If the Director returns
.B 403
the command fails clearly and prints no partial config output.
.TP
.BI "config dump -d|-n --format " yaml|json
Select config document serialization.
YAML is the default.
.TP
.BI "sessions list [--protocol " protocol "] [--backend " identifier "] [--limit " n "] [--cursor " token "] [--all]"
List active sessions, optionally filtered by protocol or backend.
Text rows include
.B holder_kind=session
for visible protocol session holders. Delivery-scoped holders remain hidden from
session list and user-session output.
The protocol filter accepts the canonical runtime protocol values such as
.BR imap ,
.BR lmtp ,
.BR sieve ,
and
.BR pop3 .
The command reads one bounded page by default.
If more records are available, text output prints
.B more=true
with the opaque
.B next_cursor
value.
Use
.B --cursor
to continue from that value, or
.B --all
to explicitly iterate pages until no cursor remains.
The default page size is configured by
.B runtime.state.indexes.page_default
and is
.B 100
by default.
The hard maximum is configured by
.B runtime.state.indexes.page_max
and is
.B 1000 .
.TP
.BI "sessions show " session-id
Show one active session.
.TP
.BI "sessions kill " session-id " --reason " text
Mark one active session for cooperative closure or report bounded absence.
The bounded session-kill states are
.B marked
,
.B missing
,
.B stale_index_repaired
and fail-closed
.B ambiguous_state .
Text output is a single scriptable line with
.B outcome
,
.B session_id
,
.B lifecycle
and
.B stale_index_repaired .
When a mark is accepted the line also includes
.B control_action=kick
and
.B control_generation .
The command exits
.B 0
for
.B outcome=marked .
If the target is already gone or a repairable stale locator was removed, the
server returns a generated
.B SessionKillResponse
with
.B outcome=missing
or
.B outcome=stale_index_repaired
and the command exits
.B 1
after printing that body.
Corrupt authoritative session state fails closed as a runtime error, commonly
.B HTTP 503 .
This is the operator-facing
.B ambiguous_state
class; scripts must treat it as a failed cleanup attempt, not as a stale-index
repair.
JSON output prints the generated response body.
.TP
.BI "users list [--limit " n "] [--cursor " token "] [--all]"
List users with runtime state.
The command reads one bounded page by default and prints
.B more=true
with an opaque
.B next_cursor
when another page may exist.
Use
.B --cursor
to continue from that value, or
.B --all
to explicitly iterate pages.
The default page size is configured by
.B runtime.state.indexes.page_default
and is
.B 100
by default.
The hard maximum is configured by
.B runtime.state.indexes.page_max
and is
.B 1000 .
.TP
.BI "users show " user-key
Show one user's runtime state.
.TP
.BI "users sessions " user-key
List sessions for one user.
Text rows use the same bounded session fields as
.BR "sessions list" ,
including
.B holder_kind=session
for visible protocol sessions.
.TP
.BI "users affinity show " user-key
Show active or retained affinity for one user.
The text output includes the shard tag, backend node, active session count,
active holder count, bounded binding source, binding status, generation and
retained-binding expiry when Redis still has an authoritative backend binding.
.TP
.BI "users affinity set " user-key " --shard " shard " --reason " text
Set user affinity for future sessions.
.TP
.BI "users affinity clear " user-key " --reason " text
Clear inactive user affinity.
.TP
.BI "users backend-pin show " user-key
Show all scoped backend pins for one user.
Text output prints one deterministic line per scoped pin, sorted by protocol and
backend pool, and includes protocol, backend pool, backend identifier, backend
node, shard tag, strategy, generation and active session count when present.
JSON output returns the generated aggregate backend-pin response body.
.TP
.BI "users backend-pin set " user-key " --backend " identifier " --strategy " strategy " --reason " text
Set a runtime backend pin for one user and one concrete configured backend.
This is intended for controlled backend commissioning, especially when a backend
is excluded from general placement by runtime weight while selected test users
must still reach it.
The target must be a configured backend identifier.
The pin is scoped to that backend's protocol and backend pool, and it derives
the protocol, backend pool, effective shard and backend node from the backend
registry instead of trusting operator input.
.TP
.BI "users backend-pin set " user-key " --backend-node " node " --strategy " strategy " --reason " text
Resolve a backend node to every configured backend-pin protocol scope and set
those pins as one runtime workflow.
The server fails without partial state if the backend node is unknown, missing a
required configured scope, resolves one scope ambiguously or maps to conflicting
shards.
.TP
.BI "users backend-pin set " user-key " --backend-node " node " --protocol " protocol " --backend-pool " pool " --strategy " strategy " --reason " text
Resolve one backend-node protocol/backend-pool scope and set only that scoped
pin.
The CLI requires
.B --protocol
and
.B --backend-pool
together for scoped backend-node requests.
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
nauthilus-directorctl users backend-pin set user@example.org \\
  --backend mailstack-canary-imap \\
  --strategy new_sessions_only \\
  --reason "commission IMAP"

nauthilus-directorctl users backend-pin set user@example.org \\
  --backend-node mailstack-canary \\
  --strategy new_sessions_only \\
  --reason "mailbox canary"

nauthilus-directorctl users backend-pin set user@example.org \\
  --backend-node mailstack-canary \\
  --protocol imap \\
  --backend-pool imap-default \\
  --strategy new_sessions_only \\
  --reason "commission IMAP"
.fi
.RE
.PP
A backend pin applies only when the user's selected shard and any active or
retained backend-node binding already match the pinned backend. Use
.B users move
for cross-shard movement, and clear inactive affinity only when a controlled
backend-node change is intended and no active sessions remain.
Backend pins can target either a weight-zero backend or an ordinary non-zero
weight backend.
They bypass only the weight-zero placement exclusion for the target backend;
runtime out, drain, maintenance, health, connection-limit and protocol/pool
safety exclusions still fail closed.
Supported strategies are
.BR new_sessions_only ,
.BR kick_existing
and
.BR drain_existing .
.TP
.BI "users backend-pin clear " user-key " --reason " text
Clear every scoped backend pin for one user without rewriting YAML
configuration.
.TP
.BI "users backend-pin clear " user-key " --protocol " protocol " --backend-pool " pool " --reason " text
Clear one protocol/backend-pool backend pin while preserving every other scoped
pin for the same user.
The CLI requires
.B --protocol
and
.B --backend-pool
together for scoped clears.
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
nauthilus-directorctl users backend-pin clear user@example.org \\
  --reason "mailbox canary done"

nauthilus-directorctl users backend-pin clear user@example.org \\
  --protocol imap \\
  --backend-pool imap-default \\
  --reason "IMAP canary done"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Clearing backend pins is explicit and does not terminate sessions, clear shard
affinity or remove a pending shard move by itself.
.TP
.BI "users hold show " user-key
Show the active placement hold for one user.
Absent or expired holds are reported as
.B present=false .
The read output includes only bounded hold metadata such as expiry, remaining
seconds, generation and creation time.
Operator reason text is not displayed.
.TP
.BI "users hold set " user-key " --duration " duration " --reason " text
Set a time-limited placement hold for one user.
The
.B --duration
value uses Go duration syntax and must resolve to a positive whole-second value
before it is sent as generated REST
.B duration_seconds .
The server enforces the configured
.B director.affinity.user_holds.max_duration
limit.
A hold is a temporary placement gate only; it does not choose a shard or backend
and it does not rewrite YAML configuration.
.TP
.BI "users hold clear " user-key " --reason " text
Clear the placement hold for one user.
Clearing a hold does not kill sessions, clear affinity, clear backend pins or
change pending move state.
The command is intended for migration workflows that first set a bounded hold,
apply a shard move or backend pin, then clear the hold when the target runtime
state is ready.
.TP
.BI "users move " user-key " --to-shard " shard " --strategy " strategy " --reason " text
Move future user placement to a shard.
This command remains shard-targeted; it does not accept a concrete backend
identifier.
Supported strategies are
.BR new_sessions_only ,
.BR kick_existing
and
.BR drain_existing .
.TP
.BI "users kick " user-key " --reason " text
Mark active sessions for one user for cooperative closure.
The operation is cooperative: it marks active sessions for the affinity key,
accelerates local stream closure when this Director owns the stream handle, and
relies on remote heartbeat observation or lease expiry for streams owned by
other replicas.
Text output reports
.B status=accepted
,
.B mark=cooperative
,
.B local_close=accelerated_when_owned
and
.B remote_close=heartbeat_or_lease .
It also prints an active-session count when the generated API response exposes
one; otherwise it prints
.B active_sessions=unknown .
JSON output stays the generated accepted response body.
.TP
.B runtime summary
Show repairable aggregate runtime totals without listing every active session.
The output is operator summary state and is not routing authority.
.TP
.BI "runtime reap --reason " text " --limit " n " --max-pass-duration " duration
Run one bounded runtime reaper pass through the existing session reaper path.
The command repairs expired session leases and repairable due/session locators,
and reports backend reservation repairs, aggregate markers removed and idle
affinities added when the reaper proves those changes.
It does not clear retained inactive affinity; use
.B users affinity clear
only after holder verification.
The
.B --reason
flag is required for audit,
.B --limit
must be positive and no larger than the server maximum, and
.B --max-pass-duration
uses a positive whole-second Go duration such as
.BR 5s .
Add
.B --dry-run
for a non-mutating preview.
Text output is a single scriptable line:
.RS
.nf
status=reaped scanned_sessions=... expired_sessions=... stale_index_entries=... repaired_backends=... aggregate_markers_removed=... idle_affinities_added=...
.fi
.RE
With
.B --dry-run
the status is
.BR preview .
JSON output prints the generated
.B RuntimeReapResponse
body.
.TP
.BI "runtime reconcile aggregates --reason " text " --limit " n " --max-pass-duration " duration
Reconcile repairable runtime aggregate marker and counter drift from
authoritative runtime state.
This command is intended for aggregate-only drift, for example when
.B runtime summary
still shows obsolete counts after
.B sessions list
and
.B users list
are clean.
It does not decide routing, authenticate credentials, clear affinity or rewrite
YAML configuration.
The
.B --reason
flag is required for audit,
.B --limit
must be positive and no larger than the server maximum, and
.B --max-pass-duration
uses a positive whole-second Go duration such as
.BR 5s .
The optional
.BI "--scope " scope
accepts
.BR all ,
.BR active_sessions ,
.BR backend_capacity ,
.BR idle_affinities
or
.BR repairs ;
omitting it sends
.BR all .
The current state repair pass reconciles active-session aggregate markers and
counters from authoritative session reads and reports authoritative conflicts
without deleting corrupt authoritative state.
Add
.B --dry-run
for a non-mutating preview.
Text output is a single scriptable line:
.RS
.nf
status=reconciled scanned_markers=... stale_markers_removed=... markers_upserted=... counter_fields_changed=... counter_fields_removed=... authoritative_conflicts=...
.fi
.RE
With
.B --dry-run
the status is
.BR preview .
JSON output prints the generated
.B RuntimeAggregateReconcileResponse
body.
.TP
.BI "route lookup --protocol imap --user " user-key
.br
.BI "route lookup --protocol sieve --user " user-key
.br
.BI "route lookup --protocol pop3 --user " user-key
.br
.BI "route lookup --protocol lmtp --recipient " address
Run a side-effect-free route lookup diagnostic.
Optional flags are
.BI "--listener " name
,
.BI "--tenant " tenant
,
.BI "--service-name " name
,
.BI "--backend-pool " pool
,
.BI "--client-ip " address
,
.B --include-affinity
and repeated
.BI "--attribute " k=v .
Use
.B --user
when the routing identity is already known.
Use
.B --recipient
for LMTP recipient diagnostics.
ManageSieve route lookup uses the canonical protocol value
.B sieve
and
.B --user
with caller-supplied user facts and never accepts script names or script
contents as routing input.
POP3 route lookup uses the canonical protocol value
.B pop3
and
.B --user
with caller-supplied user facts.
It does not accept credentials, SASL blobs, message numbers, UIDLs, message
sizes, message content or mailbox data.
LMTP recipient diagnostics may use only existing director-owned runtime state.
If the account cannot be resolved locally, the command returns a bounded
fail-closed diagnostic instead of calling Nauthilus.
Route lookup does not authenticate credentials, create sessions, refresh
leases, connect to backends or mutate runtime state.
The
.B source
field reports whether the dry-run decision came from active affinity, a
retained backend binding, an operator backend pin, a movement override, initial
placement or a fail-closed condition.
The bounded
.B reason_class
distinguishes active backend binding, retained backend binding,
backend-node protocol-entry absence, backend-node unusability,
backend-node mismatch, retained close and expiry classes without exposing raw
backend text.
With
.BR --include-affinity ,
the output includes redaction-safe binding context such as shard tag,
backend node, selected backend identifier where permitted, active holder count,
binding status and retained-binding expiry.
When a user placement hold is active, route lookup reports bounded hold context
such as presence, expiry and
.B user_hold_active
without waiting behind the hold or exposing the operator reason text.
Credential- or mailbox-bearing attributes are rejected before the request is
sent.
.TP
.B reload
Request a safe runtime reload from the running Director.
The command calls
.B POST /api/v1/reload
through the generated OpenAPI client and requires the same control-plane
authentication as other protected
.B /api/v1
operations.
Reload never rewrites YAML.
It accepts listener additions/removals and backend registry or backend-pool
changes for new sessions, and rejects restart-required classes atomically.
Existing sessions, active affinities, backend pins and placement holds are
preserved unless a separate explicit runtime command changes them.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
The request was sent through the generated client and the server-side operation
failed, or the generated client could not complete the request.
For
.B sessions kill ,
missing and stale-repaired targets also use exit status
.B 1
after printing the bounded generated response body.
.TP
.B 2
The command line or local client configuration was invalid.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nauthilus-director (1),
.BR nauthilus-director.yaml (5)
